<main>

  <h1>Release Readiness Review — checkout-service v2.4</h1>
  <p class="meta">Release manager briefing for the on-call team · Target cut: Thu 14:00 UTC · Owner: R. Mehta (release manager, accountable for the cut decision) · Reviewers: on-call primary, payments lead, and SRE lead (3 reviewers; with the release manager as the 4th approver, all 4 sign-offs are required before the cut)</p>

  <h2>1. Summary</h2>
  <p>checkout-service v2.4 is a low-risk release: it changes the card-vault retry policy from a single attempt to up to three attempts with exponential backoff and full jitter, and it adds a client-generated idempotency key to the capture path. No public API contract changes, no schema changes that require a down-migration, and no new infrastructure; if this verification is skipped and any of those conditions turns out to be false, rollback to v2.3.7 could fail or strand the API and database on an incompatible version. I am recommending we proceed with the Thursday cut, with one caveat — the eu-west card vault incident (INC-4471) is still open and shares a failure domain with the retry change.</p>
  <p>If the on-call primary disagrees with that assessment, the correct action is to block the cut, not to request a delay of the release window. A blocked cut costs us one sprint slot; a bad cut during an open incident costs us the incident.</p>

  <h2>2. Scope of change</h2>
  <p>The release contains 14 merged pull requests. The two that matter are the vault retry policy (exponential backoff, 3 attempts, 250 ms base, full jitter) and the capture idempotency key, which is now generated client-side and persisted in Redis with a 24-hour TTL. The remaining twelve are dependency bumps, log-field renames, and test-only changes.</p>
  <p>The retry change increases worst-case checkout latency by roughly 1.75 s when the vault is degraded, because three attempts at 250/500/1000 ms plus jitter stack on top of the existing 2 s client timeout. I believe this is acceptable because it only fires on vault errors, but it is the single claim in this document most worth challenging.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Blast radius</th><th>Feature flag</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Vault retry policy</td><td>All card payments, all regions</td><td><code>checkout.vault_retry_v2</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capture idempotency key</td><td>Capture path only</td><td><code>checkout.capture_idem</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 dependency / logging PRs</td><td>None observed</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Error budget status</h2>
  <p>Our availability SLO for the checkout API is 99.95% over a rolling 30-day window, which gives us 21.6 minutes of allowed unavailability. As of this morning we have consumed 8.1 minutes, leaving 62.5% of the budget. That is a comfortable position, but it is not the number I would use to justify a risky release.</p>
  <p>Two of the last seven days account for 5.4 of the 8.1 consumed minutes, and both were vault-related. The budget is healthy; the burn pattern is not.</p>
