      <text class="lbl" x="176" y="12">3.8 min</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>Daily error minutes, last 7 days. Total 8.1 min consumed of a 21.6 min monthly budget. Tuesday's 3.8 min spike was INC-4471's first occurrence.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>4. Rollback plan</h2>
  <p>Rollback is a single command, <code>deploy rollback checkout-service --to v2.3.7</code>, and takes about 4 minutes end to end based on the last two rehearsals. Both new behaviours sit behind feature flags, so the faster path for a partial problem is to disable <code>checkout.vault_retry_v2</code> and <code>checkout.capture_idem</code> in LaunchDarkly, which propagates in under 30 seconds without a deploy.</p>
  <p>There is no down-migration to run. The idempotency key is stored in Redis, not Postgres, and the Redis entries expire on their own within 24 hours; a rollback leaves orphaned keys that v2.3.7 simply ignores. I want the on-call primary to confirm they are comfortable with that claim before we cut.</p>

  <h2>5. Open incidents</h2>
  <p><strong>INC-4471</strong> <span class="pill bad">open · sev2</span> — elevated 5xx from the card vault in eu-west, ongoing for 6 days, root cause not yet identified. It is intermittent and currently affects roughly 0.4% of card authorisations in that region. This is the main reason a reviewer might reasonably block the release.</p>
  <p><strong>INC-4488</strong> <span class="pill warn">open · sev3</span> — duplicate webhook deliveries to three merchant endpoints, observed since Monday. The cause is a known consumer-side dedupe gap, not a checkout-service defect, and it is unrelated to v2.4. I do not consider it a release blocker, but I am flagging it because it will add noise to the on-call rotation during the release window.</p>

  <h2>6. Monitoring gaps</h2>
  <p>We have three known gaps that matter for this specific release. First, there is no alert on vault timeout rate — we only alert on the resulting 5xx, which means a retry storm could burn budget for several minutes before anyone is paged. Second, there is no dashboard for idempotency-key collision rate, so if the new capture key is generated incorrectly we would see duplicate captures in merchant reports rather than in our own telemetry.</p>
  <p>Third, distributed traces are sampled at 1%, which is too sparse to reconstruct a retry sequence during a low-volume incident. I am proposing we temporarily raise sampling to 10% for the 48 hours after the cut. That is a change to production config, so it needs the SRE lead's explicit approval rather than mine.</p>

  <h2>7. Sign-off</h2>
  <p>Each approver should tick their row only after reading sections 2, 4, and 6. Ticks are stored against this document and update immediately — there is no separate save step. A release with fewer than four ticks will not be cut.</p>
  <table class="signoff">
    <thead><tr><th>Approver</th><th>Question they are answering</th><th>Signed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Release manager (R. Mehta)</td><td>Is the change set complete and correctly described?</td><td><input type="checkbox" id="so-release-manager" aria-label="Release manager sign-off"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>On-call primary</td><td>Can we absorb this release alongside INC-4471?</td><td><input type="checkbox" id="so-oncall-primary" aria-label="On-call primary sign-off"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payments lead</td><td>Is the retry and idempotency behaviour correct?</td><td><input type="checkbox" id="so-payments-lead" aria-label="Payments lead sign-off"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>SRE lead</td><td>Are the monitoring gaps acceptable, and is the sampling change approved?</td><td><input type="checkbox" id="so-sre-lead" aria-label="SRE lead sign-off"></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="status" id="signoff-status" aria-live="polite">Loading sign-off state…</p>

</main>

<script>
(function () {
  var roles = ["release-manager", "oncall-primary", "payments-lead", "sre-lead"];
  var status = document.getElementById("signoff-status");

  function keyFor(role) { return "signoff:" + role; }

  function render(state) {
    var count = 0;
    roles.forEach(function (role) {
      var box = document.getElementById("so-" + role);
      var entry = state[keyFor(role)];
      var on = !!(entry && entry.approved);
      if (box) box.checked = on;
      if (on) count++;
    });
    status.textContent = count + " of " + roles.length + " approvers have signed off." +
      (count === roles.length ? " Release is cleared to cut." : " Release is not yet cleared to cut.");
  }

  function readAll() {
    if (!window.slashdocs || !window.slashdocs.kv) {
      status.textContent = "Sign-off state unavailable in this context.";
      return;
    }
    window.slashdocs.kv.all().then(function (items) {
      var state = {};
      roles.forEach(function (role) {
        var v = items[keyFor(role)];
        if (v) state[keyFor(role)] = v;
      });
      render(state);
    }).catch(function () {
      status.textContent = "Could not load sign-off state.";
    });
  }

  roles.forEach(function (role) {
    var box = document.getElementById("so-" + role);
    if (!box) return;
    box.addEventListener("change", function () {
      if (!window.slashdocs || !window.slashdocs.kv) return;
      var value = box.checked ? { approved: true, at: new Date().toISOString() } : null;
      window.slashdocs.kv.set(keyFor(role), value).catch(function () {
        status.textContent = "Could not save that sign-off. Try again.";
      });
    });
  });

  if (window.slashdocs && window.slashdocs.on) {
    window.slashdocs.on("kvchange", function () { readAll(); });
  }
  readAll();
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
